@meta (version: "1.0", title: "A growth hypothesis", locale: "en-US", currency: "USD")

# A hypothesis you can move

![Blank letter in a blue envelope](assets/ec254171e91ca507fd37cbec4b67c581c3175a89e8e08e15e0345bda68b93b11.webp)

Imagine an independent newsletter with 100 readers today. An editor wants to
understand what a steady change in readership would mean over four editions.
This fictional model is **not a forecast**: move the rate or select a point in
the chart to see the assumption and the sentence below change together.

@let starting_readers = 100
@slider growth = 5 (min: 0, max: 20, step: 1, label: "Growth per period (%)")
@slider period = 2 (min: 0, max: 4, step: 1, label: "Period to inspect")
@calc selected_readers = round(starting_readers * pow(1 + growth / 100, period))

@data steps
period:number
0
1
2
3
4
@end

@chart line steps (
  x: "period",
  y: round(starting_readers * pow(1 + growth / 100, row.period)),
  select: period,
  title: "Readers across five editions",
  xLabel: "Edition after today",
  yLabel: "Readers",
)

At period **{{ period }}**, the model gives **{{ selected_readers }} readers**.
The assumed rate is **{{ percent(growth / 100) }} per period**.

@when growth == 0
Without growth, every period has the same value.
@end

@details "What the calculation assumes"
Every period applies the same rate. There is no external data connection.

1. Begin with the initial readership.
2. Multiply by the same factor for each period.
3. Round only the displayed count in this example.

```text
The code in this block is literal:
@slider not_a_control = 5
{{ not_an_expression }}
```
@end

## Use the result carefully

The line makes a question visible: what would have to be true for this rate to
hold? A real editorial decision would need actual audience data and uncertainty,
which this small illustration does not include.
